      "problem": "$\\,$ $n$ is positive or $m$ is negative.\n\nThe negation of the composite predicate above is the following:\n\n$\\,$ \n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}{{field-3}}",
      "worked_solution": "De Morgan's law for disjunctions states that\n\n$$\n¬(A∨B)=¬A∧¬B.\n$$\n\nSo, the negation of \"$n$ is positive or $m$ is negative\" is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}¬(n\\text{ is positive or }m\\text{ is negative }) & ≡¬(n\\text{ is positive}∨m\\text{ is negative }) \\\\  & ≡¬(n\\text{ is positive})∧¬(m\\text{ is negative }) \\\\  & ≡n\\text{ is not positive}∧m\\text{ is not negative} \\\\  & ≡n\\text{ is not positive and }m\\text{ is not negative}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
